new_dynarec arm64: run on Darwin (x18, W^X code cache, cache flush)

Three things stop the arm64 recompiler on macOS; all changes are under __APPLE__.

1. x18. The recompiler allocates x18 to guest registers and uses it as the scratch
   register of its far-jump trampolines. Darwin reserves x18 and does not keep it
   across context switches (measured: lost about 1400 times a second on a busy
   machine). EXCLUDE_REG, which on arm64 was 29 and therefore excluded nothing, is
   now 18, so the allocator never hands it out, and trampolines use x17
   (TRAMPOLINE_REG), with the existing "target is in the trampoline register" moves
   keyed on that.

2. Code cache. Apple Silicon refuses pages that are writable and executable at once
   (FIXED_CACHE_ADDR), and POSIX shared memory cannot be mapped executable
   (DOUBLE_CACHE_ADDR as written dies with SIGBUS). The cache is now an anonymous
   read/write mapping plus a second, read/execute view of the same pages made with
   mach_vm_remap and laid over extra_memory, so generated code stays inside the
   library image: direct branches to the C helpers and adrp to the device state are
   always in range. extra_memory grows by one 16 KB page to hold a page-aligned
   32 MB.

3. Instruction cache. "mrs ctr_el0" and "dc civac"/"ic ivau" are replaced by
   sys_icache_invalidate.

--- a/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/arm64/assem_arm64.h
+++ b/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/arm64/assem_arm64.h
